    27	\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
    28	A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are expensive and run after the response is served.
    29	FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the response is served, at about \const{70} to \const{95} times the cost of one forward pass~\cite[p.~18]{rinberg2026flashprove}, for a window of \const{1024} tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU~\cite[pp.~16--17]{rinberg2026flashprove}. Its Lean proofs cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved~\cite[p.~17]{rinberg2026flashprove}. Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
    30	
    31	We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks~\cite{douceur2002sybil}) are outside the model.
    32	
    33	\paragraph{Checking newcomers is an old idea.} Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host's jobs until the host passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}; customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}. Checking can also depend on what a participant reports~\cite{zarkoob2020spot}; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3's persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything else is held equal?
    34	
    35	\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.
    36	
    37	\paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
    38	
    39	\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, that locate R3's advantage. It sits in the attacker's identity replacement: without replacement most of the gap to uniform audits disappears. Under honest churn, at the tested rates: R3 then spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the failed predictions, and the follow-ups' plans, to be released with the paper.
    40	% BEGIN-EV2
    41	\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets and without honest churn, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC and under D2+H3 (Section~\ref{sec:results}). Q4: the registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags. Q2 and Q3 (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results}): the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and the registered W1 search (efficiency \site{int_w1}) supplies no efficiency bound for randomised attacks. The two follow-ups are in Sections~\ref{sec:reset} and~\ref{sec:churn}.
    42	% END-EV2
    43	
    44	\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
    45	\paragraph{Verification-based probation.} Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a worker's verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; follow-on work treats imperfect checks~\cite{watanabe2010generalized}. BOINC's adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and trusts a host above a threshold~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing gold and peer evaluation~\cite{qiu2018crowdeval}. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}, and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close supervision of new firms~\cite{gangl2014supervision}. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the track record~\cite{bhaskar2025screening}. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit quota (Section~\ref{sec:rules}).
    46	
    47	\paragraph{Cheap pseudonyms.} With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price of cooperation~\cite{friedman2001social}; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high~\cite{feldman2006free}; without a central authority one attacker can create many identities~\cite{douceur2002sybil}. Reputation mechanisms with pure moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in~\cite{dellarocas2005reputation}. Reputation without persistent identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in~\cite{gatta2026tempting}; that model has no verification budget.
    48	
    49	\paragraph{Costly verification and audits.} Costly state verification goes back to~\cite{townsend1979optimal}; history-dependent audit groups under restricted penalties go back to~\cite{harrington1988enforcement}; allocation with costly verification~\cite{benporath2014optimal}, limited punishments~\cite{li2020mechanism}, financially constrained agents~\cite{li2021financially}, and ex post verification with limited penalties~\cite{mylovanov2017optimal} assume persistent agents. Recursive inspection games spend a finite stock of inspections~\cite{vonstengel2016recursive}; audit games optimise randomised inspection against a best-responding attacker~\cite{blocki2013audit}; targeted and untargeted inspection under fixed capacity~\cite{liu2013enforcement} and dynamic monitoring under resource constraints~\cite{solan2021dynamic} are also studied; optimal committed audit policies against the worst equilibrium of the audited agents, with budgeted variants, are derived in~\cite{das2026optimally}. Tournament-based dynamic targeting allocates a fixed inspection capacity by relative compliance rank and compares it with standards-based and random inspection~\cite{gilpatric2015competition}; R3 ranks by identity age instead. Our rules are searched and tuned heuristics, not optima of these models. Paid computation with verification by recomputation and colluding workers is analysed in~\cite{fernandezanta2015algorithmic}, and cheating in outsourced distributed computation in~\cite{golle2001uncheatable}.
    50	
    51	\paragraph{Verifiable computation.} Proofs that a computation was executed as committed now reach large language models. FlashProve proves, after the fact, that a committed integer version of a 400-billion-parameter model produced a token as its greedy choice; its Lean proofs cover the protocol, while the executable prover and verifier were generated by AI and are not proved equivalent to it, and its technical report states that it was AI-generated and not reviewed in detail~\cite{rinberg2026flashprove}. Such proofs check that a committed computation ran; they do not check the quality of an output, which is what our audits observe.
    52	
    53	\paragraph{Evaluation method.} Simulation-based strategic analysis has an established method~\cite{wellman2006methods,wellman2025egta}, including bootstrap inference for empirical games~\cite{wiedenbeck2014bootstrap}. Weak or incomplete attacks overstate robustness, and adaptive evaluations must be tailored to each defence~\cite{carlini2019evaluating,tramer2020adaptive}. Preregistration separates generating a hypothesis from testing it on new observations~\cite{nosek2018preregistration}. Our staged registration applies these tools.
    54	
    55	\section{The market model}\label{sec:model}
    56	\paragraph{Market.} Time is discrete, $t = 1, \dots, T$, with $T = 200$ in both tests except the first test's language-model attacker ($T = 100$). In each round $J = 50$ unit jobs arrive; job $j$ has value $v_j$ per unit of delivered quality, drawn independently and uniformly on $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}]$. There are $N = 10$ providers; provider $i$ has a quality ceiling $q^{\max}_i$, a per-job cost $a_i + b_i q$ at effort $q$, a capacity of $k_i$ jobs per round, and an outside option of zero. The provider population and the market parameters of each test are stated in Appendix~\ref{sec:eval1} and Section~\ref{sec:design}.
    57	
    58	\paragraph{Offers, effort, delivery.} In each round an active provider privately chooses an effort $q_i \in [0, q^{\max}_i]$ for all its jobs and submits a public offer: a claim $c_i \in [0,1]$ (a promise that expected delivered quality is at least $c_i$) and a per-unit bid. A served job delivers $y = q_i + \varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2)$, independently across jobs; the buyer's value is $v_j y$. Delivering below the claim is shading.
    59	
    60	\paragraph{Rounds and audits.} The rule allocates jobs and computes payments, which buyers fund into escrow. After delivery the rule selects jobs to audit; an audit reveals $y$ exactly at cost $\alpha$. A job fails if $y < c_i - \tau$, with $\tau$ the rule's tolerance. The rule imposes consequences, and escrow is settled.
    61	
    62	\paragraph{Audit budget.} Let $\hat S$ be the expected honest first-best surplus over $T$ rounds and $m = \hat S / n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator's audit budget is $B = \beta \hat S$ and the audit cost $\alpha = \rho m$, so the allowance is $B/\alpha = (\beta/\rho)\, n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ audits; unspent budget carries over.
    63	
    64	\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. In the registered tests the honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Section~\ref{sec:churn} relaxes this: honest opponents replace their identities at a fixed rate.
    65	
    66	\paragraph{Admissible rules.} A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought, its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only consequences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity's receipts as a bond; and allocation priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.
    67	
    68	\paragraph{Benchmark and metrics.} One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly at full effort with truthful claims; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best surplus $S^*$, the focal provider's \emph{gain} is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected truthful reference, divided by $S^*/N$. \emph{Gross efficiency} is $(W + \text{audit cost})/S^*$, with $W$ the realised social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is $W/S^*$. Gain is a difference of two searched values, so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity; Section~\ref{sec:reset} reports an exploratory matched comparison with replacement disabled, within the D2 family only. The second test adds two objectives at its headline market: welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker's reference on the training seeds (W1), whose profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95\% lower bound on the evaluation seeds is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain $G_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and by the worst member's mean gain $G_{\min}$, both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results say nothing about coalition stability.
    69	
    70	\section{The audit rules we compare}\label{sec:rules}
    71	\paragraph{Baselines.} B2 posts prices for three claim tiers, allocates by those prices, and audits uniformly. B3 allocates each job, in descending order of value, to the offer with remaining capacity and the highest nonnegative score $v_j c_i - \mathrm{bid}_i$, pays $v_j c_i - s_{2,j}$ with $s_{2,j}$ the highest score among the other eligible offers (zero if there is none), and audits uniformly at random among served jobs, spread over the rounds. Both refund a failed job and ban its identity for $L$ rounds.
    72	
    73	\paragraph{R3.} R3 keeps B3's allocation, payment, tolerance, and enforcement and changes only which jobs it audits and when. \emph{Pacing:} with the integer allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, R3 reserves $\min(JL, A)$ audits for the last $L$ rounds, in which it audits every served job while audits remain, and spends the rest evenly over the earlier rounds, carrying unspent quota forward. \emph{Selection:} it first audits the served jobs of identities younger than $L$ rounds, newest first; it then spends $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ of the $r$ remaining audits on the newest established identities and the rest uniformly at random.
    74	
    75	\paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
    76	
    77	\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
    78	
    79	\paragraph{The repair R4 and tuned variants.} R4 is R3 with the terminal reserve capped at $\min(JL, \lfloor A/2 \rfloor)$, and C$^*$/S2 is C$^*$ with that schedule. They differ from R3 and C$^*$ only when $JL > \lfloor A/2 \rfloor$: they equal them in the second test's blocks at $\rho = \const{0.2}$ and appear only in its repair block at $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's reserve consumes the whole allowance. Separately tuned versions of B3, R3, and C$^*$ (B3-t, R3-t, C$^*$-t), each over a common grid of \const{16} tolerance and ban-length pairs and so possibly at other pairs than the fixed one, are a secondary comparison.
    80	
    81	\section{What the theory shows}\label{sec:theory}
    82	We fix a provider facing honest opponents and an offer with claim $0 < c \le q^{\max}$, and compare truthful effort $c$ with effort $c - \Delta$, $0 \le \Delta \le c$, at the unchanged offer. We condition on the pre-delivery state, rival outcomes, and the audit set, all independent of the focal delivery noise, and assume that focal audit outcomes affect continuation value only through whether any focal job fails, with the same conditional values under both effort levels. Shading saves $g = b\Delta$ per served job and raises the per-audit failure probability from $d_0 = \Phi(-\tau/\sigma)$ to $d = \Phi((\Delta - \tau)/\sigma)$. Let $s$ be the provider's served jobs in the round, $U$ the audited subset with $K = |U|$ and escrows $M_j$, and $C_U$ the difference between the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one.
    83	
    84	\begin{lemma}[local identity]\label{lem:local}
    85	Under these assumptions, the expected profit of shading by $\Delta$ relative to truthful effort is
    86	\[ A(\Delta) = s g - \mathbb{E}\Big[(d - d_0)\textstyle\sum_{j \in U} M_j + \big((1-d_0)^K - (1-d)^K\big) C_U\Big]. \]
    87	\end{lemma}
    88	
    89	\begin{corollary}[uniform auditing under replacement]\label{cor:uniform}
    90	If, after an immediate replacement at $e = 0$, the distribution of all payoff-relevant future states equals that after no failure up to identity relabelling, and every continuation available after a failure is also available after no failure up to relabelling (both hold for B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation), then $C_U = 0$, and shading pays whenever $sg > (d - d_0)\,\mathbb{E}\sum_{j \in U} M_j$, whatever the ban length.
    91	\end{corollary}
    92	
    93	\begin{lemma}[coverage]\label{lem:coverage}
    94	R3 audits every served job of identities younger than $L$ rounds (age counted from the first offer) whenever the round's audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited by age group, newest first, with $k$ the round's available audit count; an identity that offers but wins no jobs still ages; within the focal identity's group of $n$ jobs, of which $s$ are its own, the number audited is hypergeometric with $k' = \min\{n, \max(0, k - H)\}$ draws, $H$ being the fresh jobs of strictly newer identities.
    95	\end{lemma}
    96	
    97	After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn it shares the newest positions with honest newcomers, and the bound is zero whenever a newer identity holds jobs or the quota is already spent (Section~\ref{sec:churn}).
    98	
    99	\begin{theorem}[conditional renewal bound]\label{thm:renewal}
   100	Consider immediate-replacement plans whose play decomposes into independent, identically distributed cycles of an established spell followed by a recovery. Conditional on survival, each established round has expected profit $r_E$ and failure probability $q_E > 0$; the recovery has duration $D \ge L$ with $\mathbb{E}D < \infty$, profit $R_F$, and $N_R$ replacements, with integrable rewards and charges. The long-run profit per round is $\bar\Pi = (r_E/q_E + \mathbb{E}R_F - e\,\mathbb{E}N_R)/(1/q_E + \mathbb{E}D)$. If, in addition, truthful play earns a common rate $h$ in both states and, on every recovery history, every permitted fresh action earns a conditional expected surplus of at most $\epsilon \ge 0$ over $h$, then with $e = 0$
   101	\[ \bar\Pi - h \le \epsilon + \frac{(r_E - h - \epsilon)_+}{1 + q_E L}. \]
   102	\end{theorem}
   103	
   104	The factor $1/(1 + q_E L)$ is a bound for regenerative policies that satisfy the theorem's assumptions; we do not establish those assumptions for the implemented, finite-horizon R3 process, and the theorem covers neither the adaptive attacker families nor coalitions. No reported result depends on it.
   105	
   106	\begin{proposition}[terminal shading]\label{prop:terminal}
   107	In a round with zero continuation loss, a served provider starting from a positive truthful effort gains from small same-offer shading whenever $b > v_{\max}\,\phi(\tau/\sigma)/\sigma$.
   108	\end{proposition}
   109	
   110	At the first test's parameters the right-hand side is below $\const{0.067}$ and every provider has $b \ge \const{0.22}$, so no studied rule eliminates gain; all claims are bounded-gain claims. Proofs are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}; the theory checks of the first test are in the supplement.
   111	
   112	\section{The attacks we test against}\label{sec:attacks}
   113	Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
