   188	\section{Honest identity churn}\label{sec:churn}
   189	% BEGIN-EV2
   190	In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so after the start the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. This section asks what remains of R3's advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the report: it changes no registered decision, and its intervals hold the six markets and the selected policies fixed.
   191	
   192	\paragraph{Design.} Each of the nine honest opponents retires its identity and re-enters under a fresh one with probability $p$ in each round, at $p = \const{0.01}$, \const{0.02}, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05} (mean identity lifetimes of \const{100}, \const{50}, about \const{33}, and \const{20} rounds; \site{ch_reps1}, \site{ch_reps2}, \site{ch_reps3}, and \site{ch_reps5} honest replacements per episode). For B3, R3, and C$^*$ on the six fresh markets at $\rho=\const{0.2}$ we reran the registered D2+H3 search, evaluation, and doubling check, each against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn; the reference menus were not regenerated. The rates are illustrative, not calibrated to any market. The plan and four expectations for $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} were written before any churn search, after a timing check on one market had shown the old attack's profit under R3 rising with $p$; the rates \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} were added by amendment after those results were seen, with four further expectations written before any run at these rates. Churn keeps capacity and quality fixed, but it also resets an honest provider's history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information.
   193	
   194	\paragraph{Gains and contrasts across the churn rates.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} and Figure~\ref{fig:churn} give the pooled gains and contrasts. The gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} without churn to \site{ch_r3_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$ ([\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_1}, \site{ch_d_2}, \site{ch_d_3}, and \site{ch_d_5} as $p$ rises; the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_0} without churn and \site{ch_q_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$, and then falls to \site{ch_q_2}, \site{ch_q_3}, and \site{ch_q_5}. With the frozen searches the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference changes sign between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}; with the doubled searches it is already \site{ch_d_1_d2} at \const{0.01}. The pooled B3 minus R3 gap changes sign between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05} under both search versions: at $p = \const{0.05}$ the attack earns more against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is more than three times the materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$, in C$^*$'s favour.
   195	
   196	\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} The intervals in Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} hold the six markets fixed. Treating the markets as a sample, Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-t} gives exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences for every rate and search version: every one includes zero except B3 minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the doubled searches. The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six markets, intervals that hold the selected policies fixed and omit search uncertainty, and they are not established beyond these markets. Market by market: at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}); the frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell. At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets with the frozen searches and on \site{ch_d_3_dneg} with the doubled ones, while B3 minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_q_3_neg} and \site{ch_q_3_dneg}, so its positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At $p = \const{0.02}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{ch_d_2_neg} markets: one market sets the pooled sign. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved at \const{0.01} or \const{0.02}, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselecting each rule's reference from its frozen menu under churn changes the reference in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, by at most \site{ch_resel_max} in gain.
   197	
   198	\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at $p = \const{0.01}$; Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_rep_lo_d} and \site{ch_rep_hi_d} with the doubled ones; where it stays old (\site{ch_stay_n} markets, frozen), R3 audits between \site{ch_stay_lo} and \site{ch_stay_hi} of its jobs. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
   199	
   200	\paragraph{Efficiency.} Under its selected attack, R3's gross efficiency falls from \site{ch_eff_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_eff_5} at \const{0.05} (\site{ch_eff_5d} with the doubled searches); C$^*$'s is \site{ch_eff_c5} at \const{0.05} and changes little across the rates (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); at \const{0.05} B3's is \site{ch_eff_b5} with the frozen and \site{ch_eff_b5d} with the doubled searches, and across the rates it shows no trend in $p$; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack differs. On \site{ch_eff_minm} at $p = \const{0.05}$, R3's efficiency under attack is \site{ch_eff_min}, below the registered threshold of \const{0.90}; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency condition is a no-churn result.
   201	
   202	\paragraph{Expectations.} Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are met: $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e1}''); C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.05} below its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e2}''); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at \const{0.01} (``\site{ch_e3}''); the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at \const{0.05}, \site{ch_sh_r3} against \site{ch_sh_c} and \site{ch_sh_b} (``\site{ch_e4}''); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (``\site{ch_e5}''); C$^*$ minus R3 lower at \const{0.03} than at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e6}''); $g_{\const{0.02}}(\mathrm{R3})$ below the midpoint of the no-churn and \const{0.05} values (``\site{ch_e7}''); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e8}''). The expectations were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.
   203	
   204	\paragraph{Scope.} Only one verified-history comparator, C$^*$, was run under churn; the runner-up comparator of Section~\ref{sec:results} was evaluated without churn only, so this section does not show that verified-history targeting in general is robust to churn. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates, where the comparison is unresolved, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}.
