A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. Two registered tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, compare it at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$.
Without honest churn, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every fresh market, though the registered replication passes on only \site{abs_q4_k} of six, and R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}; \site{abs_q1_conv} after exploratory continued searches of the flagged cells; exploratory $t$ interval over the six markets [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}]); in an exploratory evaluation without churn, a second verified-history comparator is also equivalent to R3 within the margin, with a direction that depends on the search version and is dominated by one market (runner-up minus R3 under D2+H3: \site{abs_ra_est}; R3 has the lower gain on \site{abs_ra_pos} of six markets). Welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, locate R3's advantage. When the attacker cannot replace its identity, most of the B3 minus R3 gap disappears (\site{abs_rs_g1} to \site{abs_rs_g0}). When honest providers also replace their identities with probability $p$ per round, R3's audits move to honest newcomers: the share of the attacker's jobs that R3 audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ (\site{abs_ch_fa1d} with the doubled searches) to \site{abs_ch_fa5} at $p = \const{0.05}$ (\site{abs_ch_fa5d}), where C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_q5} (\site{abs_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches; the frozen value includes one failed search). The comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved at $p = \const{0.01}$ or \const{0.02}, where one market sets the pooled sign, and C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions; the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}, though negative on three markets there, and is negative at \const{0.05}. At the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the rates where the comparison is unresolved, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits does not reach the highest rate. Market-level $t$ intervals include zero at every rate except for B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} with the doubled searches, so the reversals are not established beyond the six markets. \site{abs_ch_inc} of the \site{abs_ch_cells} churn doubling checks are flagged incomplete.
The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn rates, and one comparator under churn; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
