9        Where the advantage lives: identity replacement
Identity replacement and the B3–R3 gap (exploratory follow-up). D2+H3 is D2 plus one
switch x that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the
headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves 0.307 more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and
0.012 more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is 0.302 under D2+H3 and 0.007 under D2. To
see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget
cut 3 had removed (B3, R3, and C∗ at ρ = 0.2, each with a doubling check), under a plan and
expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six
fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent
replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights,
the B3 minus R3 gap is 0.173 (95% paired seed-bootstrap interval [0.172, 0.175]) under D2+H3 and
−0.009 ([−0.011, −0.008]) under D2 (doubled searches: 0.170 and −0.004). The share of the gap
that disappears without replacement, S = 1−G(D2)/G(D2+H3), is 1.053 (doubled searches: 1.022).
It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero
conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory t interval across the
six market means, [−0.036, 0.018], includes zero; the gap is positive on 3 of the six markets (4
with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, S
is 1.000). We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood.
The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives S from
0.967 to 1.093 over both search versions, and Table 5 lists the gaps per market. The same holds
for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C∗ minus R3 gain difference is 0.021 under D2+H3 and −0.003
under D2 (t interval [−0.018, 0.012]; doubled searches [−0.026, 0.019]). The difference between the
two prescribed searches, I = g(D2+H3) − g(D2), is 0.228 against B3, 0.045 against R3, and 0.069
against C∗ ; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance
score as follows. I(B3) > I(R3) on every market: “met” (6 markets with the frozen searches, 6 with
the doubled ones). I(C∗ ) > I(R3) on every market: “not established (search-sensitive)” (5 and 6
markets; fresh3 gives −0.041 with the frozen searches). Pooled S > 0.5: “met”. Within the D2
family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under
the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. Appendix A gives
the knob-level checks, the sensitivity to references that replace identities, and the search flags of
this follow-up; we keep the frozen searches as primary.




