Table 6: Exploratory follow-up, after the report: the D2+H3 gain g against R3, C∗ , and B3, and
the contrasts d = C∗ minus R3 and q = B3 minus R3, pooled over the six fresh markets with equal
weights, when each honest opponent replaces its identity with probability p per round; p = 0 is the
registered no-churn result on the same seeds. Paired seed-bootstrap percentile intervals; frozen and
doubled searches.

Efficiency. Under its selected attack, R3’s gross efficiency falls from 0.989 at p = 0.01 to 0.967
at 0.05 (0.949 with the doubled searches); C∗ ’s is 0.993 at 0.05 and changes little across the rates
(Table 8); at 0.05 B3’s is 0.940 with the frozen and 0.959 with the doubled searches, and across the
rates it shows no trend in p; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack
differs. On fresh2 at p = 0.05, R3’s efficiency under attack is 0.893, below the registered threshold
of 0.90; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency
condition is a no-churn result.

Expectations. Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are
met: g0.05 (R3) above its no-churn value (“met”); C∗ minus R3 at 0.05 below its no-churn value
(“met”); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at 0.01 (“met”); the audited share of honest
replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C∗ and B3 at 0.05, 0.571 against
0.356 and 0.250 (“met”); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (“met”); C∗ minus R3
lower at 0.03 than at 0.02 (“met”); g0.02 (R3) below the midpoint of the no-churn and 0.05 values
(“met”); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at 0.02 (“met”). The expectations
were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.

Scope. Only one verified-history comparator, C∗ , was run under churn; the runner-up comparator
of Section 8 was evaluated without churn only, so this section does not show that verified-history
targeting in general is robust to churn. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3’s mea-
sured advantage over C∗ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates, where the comparison is
unresolved, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through p = 0.03 but not to 0.05.


11        What the results show and what they do not
What the two tests support together. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn,
the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled
market, with R3’s registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule’s
frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities
beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section 9, which holds the parameters and search
budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not
establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the


